<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Keys merged into the generated Info.plist by tauri-bundler
  (bundle.macOS.infoPlist). User keys OVERRIDE generated ones, so keep this
  file to the minimum we actually need — anything added here shadows whatever
  Tauri would have produced.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Opt out of App Nap.

    This app is not a foreground-only UI. It hosts the loopback relay that
    carries the agent's node traffic: the backend pushes a connect.challenge
    down over the API Gateway socket THIS process owns, and it forwards the
    frame to the bundled openclaw-host subprocess, which signs it. Backgrounded
    and idle, macOS demotes us to the background QoS band and that forwarding
    stalls for seconds.

    Measured on a live 0.4.53 install (2026-08-03), scheduling priority:

        backgrounded  lightbulb-desktop = 4    openclaw-node = 31
        foregrounded  lightbulb-desktop = 46   openclaw-node = 31

    Only the relay is throttled; the signer is unaffected. Prod API Gateway
    logs caught the consequence — a challenge pushed at 23:08:23 and signed at
    23:08:36, 13s later, with backend integrationLatency of 111ms. The whole
    delay was this process waiting to be scheduled.

    The backend budget for that round trip is 30s (raised from 10s, which had
    turned a routine background app into a permanent reconnect flap), but the
    budget is tolerance, not the fix. A relay for a remote agent must stay
    responsive while the window is behind something else, which is the normal
    case for this app.

    NOT NSActivityIdleSystemSleepDisabled: this suppresses App Nap only and
    deliberately does not hold the machine awake.

    THIS KEY IS NOT WHAT HOLDS. macOS 26 ignores it from a bundle: on
    2026-10-07 runningboardd set "AppNap state: active:Y" on an install that
    carried it, six times in 26 minutes, and the session connection stalled
    for up to 112 s. What holds is the NSProcessInfo activity
    (.userInitiatedAllowingIdleSystemSleep) that `refuse_app_nap` in lib.rs
    begins at launch and never ends. The key stays for a macOS that reads it.
  -->
  <key>NSAppSleepDisabled</key>
  <true/>
  <!--
    Apple Events: Google/Apple sign-in hands off to Safari through osascript
    (lib.rs, oauth-intercept). Under the hardened runtime that needs the
    automation.apple-events entitlement AND this string; without the string
    macOS refuses the event with no prompt (-1743).
  -->
  <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Lightbulb opens sign-in pages in Safari and runs the commands you let your agents run on this Mac.</string>
  <!--
    Files and folders: approved folders (file_access.rs) and the agent's shell
    run as this app, so macOS asks on first touch of these locations. The
    strings replace the generic prompt text with what the access is for.
  -->
  <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Lightbulb lets your agents read and write files in the folders you approve in Settings.</string>
  <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Lightbulb lets your agents read and write files in the folders you approve in Settings.</string>
  <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Lightbulb lets your agents read and write files in the folders you approve in Settings.</string>
  <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Lightbulb lets your agents read and write files in the folders you approve in Settings.</string>
  <key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Lightbulb lets your agents read and write files in the folders you approve in Settings.</string>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Lightbulb uses the microphone for voice calls with your agents.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
